[{:seon.task/name "a-cold-node-decodes-in-one-pass"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-10: the first read of a stored index node costs about 610-750 ticks and 2 KB a datom (69 M of a 94 M-tick scan of 113k ``:form/refs`` datoms was cold decode; the second scan 9.7 M); one konserve read of a small branch head costs 11.7k ticks and 33 KB; the fasl decode of the 17 MB reach graph 37 M ticks. It is the floor under a root's boot (seon.db/preload! (deleted) is 1.2 s of every boot and is decode work), every law that reads the program, the first scan in any test process, and each open. Should cost: at most about 100 ticks and 250 B a datom: its share of one zstd frame, five fields read, one object built (a four-field deftype is 8 ticks and 128 B on the Jolt at upstream's head). Suspected by reading, not measured: a node is decoded into generic vectors and the datoms are built from those afterwards; the leaf's array is grown, not made at its known length; keywords are interned again for each datom. First step: one counted measure of a cold scan split into decompress, fasl read and datom construction, which names the largest; no design before that number. Where: `konserve.jolt.fasl-store`, the node read handlers in `datahike.index.persistent-set`, the sorted set's `restore-node`. Proof when built: a cold scan of n datoms costs a stated number per datom, at sizes to 1,000."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.index.persistent-set-test/a-cold-node-decodes-in-one-pass}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-collections-mark-costs-what-changed", :seon.task/plan "After the one-read mark (deps, 2026-11-14), one boot's mark of the standing store (64 branches, the cutoff of seon.db/retain!) still reads 328,440 nodes raw (11.5 GB, 2.1B ticks, 28 s wall) to find 9,152 keys to sweep: it reads every reachable node at every collection, though a node never changes at its address, so the out-of-line values it references and its children stay what the last collection found. Design with runtime, the collection's caller: a mark proportional to what changed (the nodes written since the last collection, and the commits since its cutoff), the found references kept with the store so a node read once is not read again; and whether a collection's reads may run in root's process at all, beside its working set. Also: konserve's read-immutable copies the value bytes three times (~6 KB of a read's ~35 KB). Done: a boot's mark reads the nodes written since the last one, measured against these numbers. docs/storage.md#collection.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-collection-reads-only-the-nodes-written-since-the-last}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-large-value-reads-after-its-connection-is-released"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. FOUND (data, 2026-11-16): seon.db-test/a-large-value-holding-ratios-commits-and-reads-back errored :lmdb-env-closed once in a seon.db-test batch at a fresh agent (result/caaadaa4); it passed alone twice and in a namespace rerun (result/c0ea99e1). HYPOTHESIS (deps): the leaf fix (Datahike ea03e180) reads a large out-of-line value on first use, through a live connection to its store (datahike.datom/read-values-with!); before it, the value was read when its leaf was decoded. A db value kept past its connection's d/release, then read for a large value whose leaf the node cache did not hold decoded, finds the env closed. That makes it order-dependent. Proof, red first: a db value of a released connection reads a large value it never read before. Settle at the cause whether a released connection's db reads values (it reads nodes today, from the cache or the store), never by a guard."
